Even philosophers who deny libertarian free will (Dennett, Nagel) maintain that reactive attitudes — gratitude, resentment, indignation — are appropriate responses to human actions, because they are the social fabric that makes cooperative life possible.
Children are taught to deliberate carefully and act on reasons precisely because we believe this training shapes their future choices — which implies we believe their reasoning genuinely influences their actions.
The criminal justice system's move from purely retributive to rehabilitative models is predicated on the belief that people can change through learning and treatment — a belief that requires a meaningful sense of agency.
Moral luck — the observation that outcomes partly depend on factors outside our control — is troubling precisely because it conflicts with our intuition that responsibility should track what agents actually controlled.
The neuroscientific evidence that Libet interpreted as undermining free will has been substantially reinterpreted: subsequent studies show the 'readiness potential' precedes conscious awareness of urge but not the point at which the decision is revocable.
